out  a game of inches when we need to be  thinking about miles and miles and miles  so as we think about movement building  i don't know if anybody ever read the  book by jim collins called good to great  well jim collins is a stanford professor  and  his research shows highly effective  organizations  one of the great attributes about them  is they are willing to face the brutal  facts  they're willing to look at the hard  truths and face those but with an  undying certainty that they'll succeed  so as movements it plays out the same  way and we've got  two hard truths that we got to face  one there is no way that you build  a more just and sustainable economy  without directly  challenging the power of wall street and  big corporations in this country  there's simply no way to get from a to b  without changing our relationship with  corporations and as we've learned in the  fight  for health care reform as we've learned  in the fight for financial reform  and as we've learned in the fight for  climate change  that until we reclaim our democracy from  big corporations we cannot begin to  reshape our economy  hard truth number two a new and just  economy that does not address  generations of structural racism is not  a new and just economy  and as we've seen day in and day out  particularly over the last two years but  for generations the other side is more  than willing to talk about race  which means we spend our time talking  about race on defense  well i have never seen anybody win just  on defense  so we gotta pivot and say we're gonna go  on offense we're going to talk about  race  and when we envision a more just and  sustainable economy  it's going to be designed to address  generations of structural racism  so i got to go back to this slide see if  i can get this thing up there we go nice  so you know it's easy oh let's build a  movement here's the five-point plan  well i think what we want to think about  is how do you build the conditions for  movement and this is how we think about  it in national people's action  we think first you have to ground your  set and  yourself in a core set of ideas the  principles that will be at the  foundation of a movement  that'll form our moral compass to help  guide us moving forward  and we're talking about a shift  movements make a shift  to where their allegiance is to a set of  ideas not to one political party  and not to organizational affiliation  and until we make that shift put a stake  in the sand around a set of ideas  we will be pulled to political parties  because as we've seen  right now there is one big narrative and  one big idea that has traction in this  country  an anti-government big idea narrative  and it's not only pulling republicans to  the right  it's pulling democrats to the right so  we've got to get clear on what our ideas  are  second alignment effective movements  have major breakthroughs  in getting people to work together as  the progressive sector we have  lots of the capacities that we need to  actually do the right thing and to be  powerful and be effective  but at this point we're not working well  enough together so what would it look  like to  us have a serious plan to focus on  collaboration  on uh alignment around building a more  progressive and thoughtful  infrastructure  and focusing on more thoughtful uh  coordination  and the three biggest barriers we face  to this are credit control and money  that's where things break down between  organizations we want the credit we want  the control we want the money  well i don't think anywhere in anybody's  mission statement in this room does it  say  fight for credit control and money so  we've got to change that  third narrative the anti-government  narrative  that has traction in this country is one  of the biggest challenges we face  there's just no question about it laying  the biggest problems with the country at  the feet of government  has traction in this country and it is a  worldview fight that we have to  change so and i think this is one of the  biggest challenges to the new economy  that i think fader may have said it as  well as anybody  what is our inspiring compelling hopeful  narrative  about the economy and i think it needs  to include two things one  a critique around the current economy  and it has to have a critique around  free markets and corporations  and then secondly it has to have a  hopeful inspiring vision that people can  stick with for the long haul  because as we've found people will come  out join the fight  hit the streets initially out of anger  but they keep showing up year after year  out of hope  so we got to spell out the hope but the  main thing i want to focus on is  campaigns  because movements come out of action  and i think there are three things three  criteria we need to see in the kind of  campaigns and action that we move if  we're going to build a more just and  sustainable economy  and one we need to do things that expand  the realm of what's possible  too many of the fights we're engaged in  are about  winning as much as you can within the  margins as they are set  if the left is over here we're going to  push up against to the margin and do as  good as we can here and the right's  trying to push over here on this side of  the margin  but we need to blow up the margins we  need to do campaigns and do actions that  actually expand the realm of what's  possible  create new space for a new set of ideas  new ideas around strategy new ideas  around  tactics and new ideas around what the  future of the economy looks like because  right now  the ideas that we care so much about are  not on the table  secondly we have to make our problem  their problem i was just in iowa with  chris hayes of the nation speaking to a  bunch of family farmers  and chris laid out this an incredible  speech something we've always called  take the crisis to those who created it  but i think chris says it better  make our problem their problem tons of  people in this country are feeling an  economic crisis right now  tons of people but a small percentage is  completely immune from the crisis  they don't feel it in their gated  communities in their big buildings on  wall  street so we need to figure out how are  we going to take  the crisis to them how do we make our  problem their problem which means  going to people's homes which means  taking over their buildings  and putting new pressure on them and  thirdly we have to invest  in campaigns and actions that start at  the local level because if a look at  movement history  the great things that captured the  imagination of the american people  were not one big march or one big action  but they were actually  activities that created real struggle  and  unfolding drama that people could follow  day in  and day out and watch it like a soap  opera or watch it like the stock market  or watch it like their favorite sports  team and tune in so that's what we need  to do and then i want to come back  to our folks so here's an example of us  bringing the crisis to them  in 2008 you probably didn't hear about  this but we took  hundreds of people to ben bernanke's  house it was the week after they  uh bailed out bear stearns and we said  hey why don't you show the same vigor  that you're demonstrating and bailing  out bear stearns to begin to bail out  the american people so we went to his  home  we actually weren't 100 sure was it was  his address but as we got off the bus  and security jumped out of bushes we  said okay this is somebody bad did  something here  we've got somebody bad lives here so it  was good and it was bernanke's house  we then took 500 people to his office  the next day were met by armed security  but the end of the story is we've had  three negotiating meetings with chairman  bernanke  that every day poor people from across  this country have sat across from the  man  and the board of governors and pushed  him to do more to hold banks 