opulist sentiment that is a simmer at  this moment  but we can blow it up into a boil the  right now man  plant means creating the arena for  people to move to action  to tune into their sense of somebodiness  and put a new level of pressure on those  who created the crisis we face today  thank you  okay so we're down to our final speaker  and  thank you guys all for being here and  hanging in this has been a really great  session i hope that you've enjoyed  it our next speaker and our final  speaker is deepak bhargava  deepak is executive director of center  for community change based in washington  d.c  he is a very inspiring leader to me um i  know him and i think he's great and he's  been at the center of the fight for  social justice and economic justice  policy change for over a decade most  recently he's been very active in the  fight for immigration reform  and for job creation he really is one of  the rising stars of the movement  and he has a great gift of kind of  wrapping it all together and breaking it  all down and so  i know you're going to love what he has  to say right now thanks a lot and  welcome deepak  good afternoon everybody  so i'm getting the feeling that one of  the main criteria for getting to speak  at netroots this is my first  is a prominent position on the glenn  beck wall  so i'm a little concerned about my own  status  for a while i was to the right of van  jones to the left of valerie jarrett and  underneath mount saitung  but i think i'm dropping so i'm hoping  my performance today kind of helps boost  my  boost my ratings so i'm deepak bhargava  i'm a community organizer and i'm proud  of it  what i'm going to try to do today i'm  going to try out some new material with  you today  is to talk about what might be some  principles  for a long-term progressive economic  vision  but before i do that i do want to talk  about a couple of things in the here and  now  so john maynard keynes the great  economist once said when  he was told that experts were expressing  confidence that in the long run  the economy would return to equilibrium  and full employment  kane said in the long run we're all dead  so we do live in a massive economic  crisis  and the first thing i want to say is we  need jobs desperately  we need a lot of them and we need them  right now  now the good news is we actually know  how to do this  we have had the experience in our  history of the 1930s with the works  progress administration the civilian  conservation corps  in the 1970s on a smaller scale with the  ceta program  and today there's a proposal that my  organization has been working on  uh that congressman george miller is  sponsored with 160 co-sponsors in the  house  that would create a hot would create a  million jobs a year for two years  for people to do useful work in  communities  greening the economy taking care of our  kids educating our kids  refurbishing schools and senator brown  sherrod brown is sponsored in the senate  we have the capacity in in the great  depression hundreds of thousands of jobs  were created in the span of months by  fdr and harry hopkins his key aid  in a time when there weren't it wasn't  the level of federal bureaucracy and  infrastructure that there is today  so we absolutely have the capacity to do  it and we should  the second thing i want to say is i want  to challenge you and ask you all  to join me and many many other  organizations who are calling a major  mobilization in washington  on october 2nd 2010 called the one  nation march  now i'm asking you to do this not just  because marching is fun  and i do think marching is fun but  because at this moment it is of great  strategic  importance there is a kind of malaise  and despondency and dispiritedness in  many parts of the progressive world  we have been barely able to get an  unemployment benefits extension through  the united states senate  let alone the kinds of ambitious wpa  style programs that i'm suggesting that  we really need to get the economy moving  without major mobilization we will not  be able  to turn the politics around on the  economy in this country so i want you to  come and to promote  10 to 10 as a major event  so let me move on to talk about the long  run so i work at the center for  community change whose mission is  to spur and support grassroots  organizing mostly in poor communities  and in communities of color  and we've done some great things over  the years but the hard truth of the  matter is  that the last 40 years since my  organization was founded in 1968  we've been living in a period of  retrenchment and reaction  on some basic issues of racial justice  of economic justice the gains of the new  deal the great society the civil rights  movement  have been steadily eroded over that  period of time  we've had a couple of important  victories in this last two years  uh the stimulus program the recovery act  though it's not  widely understood was the largest  expansion of anti-poverty programs in  this country in 40 years  the health care bill was the largest  redistribution  redistributive program in this country  in 40 years and the largest expansion of  the safety net  but the hard truth remains that for the  people who are the reason for my  organization's existence  life is worse not better we have 20  percent unemployment in communities of  color  in this country in some neighborhoods  it's a catastrophic levels of 50 percent  or higher  we have rising poverty extreme poverty  child poverty  increasing hunger increasing  homelessness there is immense  suffering in the united states of  america and  the brutal fact is that there's almost  nothing  in the mainstream economic or political  discourse today that would do a damn  thing about  that's the hard reality we start from  so in light of the seriousness of this  predicament  i want to take a crack at trying to  define what might that long-term vision  for a progressive economics in this  country be and i start from the premise  that we carry an albatross around our  necks  which is free market fundamentalism an  ideology that is so pervasive  that it is like the air we breathe and  the water we swim in we barely recognize  it  and as the british historian uh perry  anderson  said without a conceptual alternative  across the full range from the  philosophical to the technical to the  rally political  we will not be able to break through and  make progress  and some questions in our society have  been actually put beyond the pale of  acceptable discourse how much  wealth is too much wealth how much  inequality  is okay these questions are barely asked  anymore in our society and it was  not always so and it is also the case  that this is not just a problem of  conservative  ideology when a democratic president as  bill clinton  did signed welfare reform and destroyed  the safety net for poor families in this  country  and signed nafta to send millions of  american jobs overseas and at the same  time manage to impoverish  mexican people on the land in mexico  fueling the immigration that we see now  in our country  you know that this ideology is pervasive  and it is possible that in 2011 we will  have a fight  not just with republicans but with  democrats about saving and preserving  social security  so we have a deep deep ideological  problem  and i think that fighting these fights  simply on  issue grounds trade over here deficits  over there  safety nets over here jobs over there is  not sufficient to the task at hand  that the place to begin is at the level  of fundamental principles  now we all know the conservative  principles that operate through each and  every issue fight  that we see in congress and around the  country that  human beings are basically selfish that  competition  is the currency of american life that  wealth is created  principally by individual entrepreneurs  who are heroic  and that we all benefit when they do  well  that free markets are the best way to  organize an economy  that smallest government is the best  government  that we live in a colorblind society and  that racism is essentially  a thing of the past as we say as we new  yorkers say  forget about it what's an alternative  what's a path forward so i want to lay  out six potential  principles that we might think about and  some of these i hope will be a little  provocative  so first that wealth is not created  by individual heroic entrepreneurs  but wealth is created by all of us  so that activities that we sometimes  think about is  marginal or irrelevant economic  production scholarship  art science child care care giving these  are in fact  fundamentally constitutive of the  project of wealth building  so sometimes liberals will say well the  market system is the best we got we got  to support those entrepreneurs  but let's just take a little bit of that  money and give it to the poorest people  because the outcomes are so unfair  what i'm proposing is a different view  which is not that  they are the uh geese that lay the  golden eggs but that in fact  wealth is created by everyone in our  society that we all have a contribution  to it  and that we all therefore have a moral  stake in wealth  that is not a matter of charity that it  is a matter of recognition  of the productivity of workers of  communities of everybody and creating  wealth  second principle our shared  quality of life is more important than  the private accumulation of wealth  so sometimes the pursuit of private  accumulation of wealth serve social ends  and in that case it should be rewarded  but the speculative frenzy of the  housing bubble  uh the subprime crisis the ma the  excessive salaries and bonuses of  executives  this is not fundamentally productive  activity  capital that was locked up in lavish  mansions  in luxury vehicles and financial  speculation could have been used for  education  for transportation for clean energy and  other investments  that would have laid the groundwork for  long-term growth private wealth should  be respected  but it is not sacrosanct  third human beings are complex  and they are not exclusively driven by  selfishness  now progressives are not naive we don't  assume that people are always good  we did live with dick cheney for eight  years right so we have some evidence of  the contrary but we do assert and new  science backs this up that people  are capable of acting morally and with  care and compassion  for one another we are also unapologetic  in asserting that we have  obligations deep moral obligations  to one another to act and behave as  though other people  count as much as we do from these  principles we would generate a pretty  powerful critique of what's going on in  our country  today is it okay that some people are  walking around with a three million  dollar  gold ipod while other people are living  lives of quiet and invisible desperation  in our society  i think it's a moral outrage  and i think these kinds of behaviors of  the excess of wealth  are fundamentally sociopathic  they are destructive of a good society  and they ought to be condemned and  subject to cultural critique  fourth inequality  in wealth and income beyond a certain  point and we crossed this point long ago  in the united states  is destructive not just of democracy and  of a sense of a common project  but it also undermines the prospects of  a well-functioning economy that is to  say  more equality is good for  growth for a functioning  economy and that the inequality that  we've seen is not only  excessive bad on moral grounds  it actually undermines the conditions  for a good economy  fifth racial inequality is not a  separate issue from economic inequality  they are part and parcel of the same set  of structures that operate in the united  states of america  and any progressive project on economic  inequality given the  vast disparities of wealth and income  that we still see  will have to put 