 and so forth so and the  projects of having an intellectual  agenda and ideas agenda on the one hand  and having a movement strategy on the  other are closely intertwined and linked  because if we don't have a story that we  believe in we can't build  a mass army for these changes but if we  don't have  a movement in the country we won't have  the power and we won't have the ideas  and the passion and the energy  from the grassroots to get it done so  i've had the luxury and the privilege  over the last  uh 10 years of being part of the  immigrant rights movement and i've  learned two things from that  one is that you've got a dream big when  we started no one thought that  legalizing 12 million undocumented  people was remotely feasible and not a  single person in the beltway wanted to  talk about it  similarly these kinds of economic  questions are going to need to be put on  the table  secondly we're going to need  organizations at scale  unions community groups perhaps new  forms of organization that we can't even  imagine today  to create the power and the engine for  those reforms  so let me just say in wrapping up that  um  i know this has seemed like this seems  in a way like a very perilous time  for the country and it is not going to  be easy to get some of the kinds of  reforms that were talked about here  today  but the truth is if it was easy it would  not be worthy of the progressive promise  because the progressive promise is  basically about keeping faith with the  unemployed worker in detroit the single  mom in fresno  the immigrant worker at a meat plant in  omaha with the belief  and the conviction that they are not  simply victims  but that we need their talent their  inspiration  linked together with all of us to build  a better america and we can do it and we  will do it  thank you very much  okay hi i've acquired a growth because  my husband had to go to his session this  is marco  and i think he's the youngest net roost  nation attendee ever