 more  years  all that concern became much more of a  worry when 30 percent of her pension got  wiped out after the big  meltdown next mitzi river singleton  wichita kansas single mother struggling  to keep the lights on went to get a  payday loan  and then she got into the terrible  vicious cycle of debt that makes up  payday lending  the average three hundred dollar payday  loan cost people 750 dollars  and mitzi pelta paid a hell of a lot  more than that by the time she was done  and trenda kennedy right now today trend  is fighting tooth and nail with bank of  america to save her home  she sent her papers to get her loan  modified  into bank of america 10 times and 10  times they've lost them  and then recently her son died in a car  crash  on the day she buried her son bank of  america called to collect  she said hey today is not the day but  they kept pushing  and pushing and pushing and any day now  trendy could lose her home  but the fact that these women have went  from being  a victim of the economic crisis to  fighters  in a movement for something bigger and  better speaks to a progressive  populist sentiment that though you  wouldn't hear about it on the media  is alive and well in this country  so i've moved too quick into these  slides  as we begin to think about building a  movement  we've got a tough question to ask  ourselves as we think about rebuilding  the economy  we have to decide are we going to try to  win an advanced  a few ideas to make this economy a  little bit more just a little bit more  sustainable  are we ready to say hey it's time to  chart a new course  we got to think bigger about what we're  going to do we got to  put a stake in the sand as progressives  and say  hey we are not here to tinker  and if we are not here to tinker if we  are here to think big  we got to know it will take a movement  anything less will not get it done  anything less means we're eking out  victories along the margins  anything less means we're talking about  a game of inches when we need to be  thinking about miles and miles and miles  so as we think about movement building  i don't know if anybody ever read the  book by jim collins called good to great  well jim collins is a stanford professor  and  his research shows highly effective  organizations  one of the great attributes about them  is they are willing to face the brutal  facts  they're willing to look at the hard  truths and face those but with an  undying certainty that they'll succeed  so as movements it plays out the same  way and we've got  two hard truths that we got to face  one there is no way that you build  a more just and sustainable economy  without directly  challenging the power of wall street and  big corporations in this country  there's simply no way to get from a to b  without changing our relationship with  corporations and as we've learned in the  fight  for health care reform as we've learned  in the fight for financial reform  and as we've learned in the fight for  climate change  that until we reclaim our democracy from  big corporations we cannot begin to  reshape our economy  hard truth number two a new and just  economy that does not address  generations of structural racism is not  a new and just economy  and as we've seen day in and day out  particularly over the last two years but  for generations the other side is more  than willing to talk about race  which means we spend our time talking  about race on defense  well i have never seen anybody win just  on defense  so we gotta pivot and say we're gonna go  on offense we're going to talk about  race  and when we envision a more just and  sustainable economy  it's going to be designed to address  generations of structural racism  so i got to go back to this slide see if  i can get this thing up there we go nice  so you know it's easy oh let's build a  movement here's the five-point plan  well i think what we want to think about  is how do you build the conditions for  movement and this is how we think about  it in national people's action  we think first you have to ground your  set and  yourself in a core set of ideas the  principles that will be at the  foundation of a movement  that'll form our moral compass to help  guide us moving forward  and we're talking about a shift  movements make a shift  to where their allegiance is to a set of  ideas not to one political party  and not to organizational affiliation  and until we make that shift put a stake  in the sand around a set of ideas  we will be pulled to political parties  because as we've seen  right now there is one big narrative and  one big idea that has traction in this  country  an anti-government big idea narrative  and it's not only pulling republicans to  the right  it's pulling democrats to the right so  we've got to get clear on what our ideas  are  second alignment effective movements  have major breakthroughs  in getting people to work together as  the progressive sector we have  lots of the capacities that we need to  actually do the right thing and to be  powerful and be effective  but at this point we're not working well  enough together so what would it look  like to  us have a serious plan to focus on  collaboration  on uh alignment around building a more  progressive and thoughtful  infrastructure  and focusing on more thoughtful uh  coordination  and the three biggest barriers we face  to this are credit control and money  that's where things break down between  organizations we want the credit we want  the control we want the money  well i don't think anywhere in anybody's  mission statement in this room does it  say  fight for credit control and money so  we've got to change that  third narrative the anti-government  narrative  that has traction in this country is one  of the biggest challenges we face  there's just no question about it laying  the biggest problems with the country at  the feet of government  has traction in this country and it is a  worldview fight that we have to  change so and i think this is one of the  biggest challenges to the new economy  that i think fader may have said it as  well as anybody  what is our inspiring compelling hopeful  narrative  about the economy and i think it needs  to include two things one  a critique around the current economy  and it has to have a critique around  free markets and corporations  and then secondly it has to have a  hopeful inspiring vision that people can  stick with for the long haul  because as we've found people will come  out join the fight  hit the streets initially out of anger  but they keep showing up year after year  out of hope  so we got to spell out the hope but the  main thing i want to focus on is  campaigns  because movements come out of action  and i think there are three things three  criteria we need to see in the kind of  campaigns and action that we move if  we're going to build a more just and  sustainable economy  and one we need to do things that expand  the realm of what's possible  too many of the fights we're engaged in  are about  winning as much as you can within the  margins as they are set  if the left is over here we're going to  push up against to the margin and do as  good as we can here and the right's  trying to push over here on this side of  the margin  but we need to blow up the margins we  need to do campaigns and do actions that  actually expand the realm of what's  possible  create new space for a new set of ideas  new ideas around strategy new ideas  around  tactics and new ideas around what the  future of the economy looks like because  right now  the ideas that we care so much about are  not on the table  secondly we have to make our problem  their problem i was just in iowa with  chris hayes of the nation speaking to a  bunch of family farmers  and chris laid out this an incredible  speech something we've always called  take the crisis to those who created it  but i think chris says it better  make our problem their problem tons of  people in this country are feeling an  economic crisis right now  tons of people but a small percentage is  completely immune from the crisis  they don't feel it in their gated  communities in their big buildings on  wall  street so we need to figure out how are  we going to take  the crisis to them how do we make our  problem their problem which means  going to people's homes which means  taking over their buildings  and putting new pressure on them and  thirdly we have to invest  in campaigns and actions that start at  the local level because if a look at  movement history  the great things that captured the  imagination of the american people  were not one big march or one big action  but they were actually  activities that created real struggle  and  unfolding drama that people could follow  day in  and day out and watch it like a soap  opera or watch it like the stock market  or watch it like their favorite sports  team and tune in so that's what we need  to do and then i want to come back  to our folks so here's an example of us  bringing the crisis to them  in 2008 you probably didn't hear about  this but we took  hundreds of people to ben bernanke's  house it was the week after they  uh bailed out bear stearns and we said  hey why don't you show the same vigor  that you're demonstrating and bailing  out bear stearns to begin to bail out  the american people so we went to his  home  we actually weren't 100 sure was it was  his address but as we got off the bus  and security jumped out of bushes we  said okay this is somebody bad did  something here  we've got somebody bad lives here so it  was good and it was bernanke's house  we then took 500 people to his office  the next day were met by armed security  but the end of the story is we've had  three negotiating meetings with chairman  bernanke  that every day poor people from across  this country have sat across from the  man  and the board of governors and pushed  him to do more to hold banks accountable  so making our problem their problem  works  secondly mitzi river singleton who you  saw earlier who got screwed by a payday  lender  well mitzi and 5000 people came to the  uh  chicago for the american bankers  association they were having their  convention to celebrate all their great  achievements  well we decided we needed to crash this  party they were not coming to our city  we're headquartered in chicago we had to  crash this party  and one of the great stories was brenda  leblanc another 81 year old retiree from  iowa  she took 200 people past security  because they went down  they put their sunday best on and our  folks sunday best is not  does not look like banker wear but we  got through security and went down to  michigan avenue  and got more uh nordstrom bags banana  republic bags  and gucci bags we didn't buy anything we  just asked for the bags  and we walked straight through security  took over the lobby  occupied the building and put a new  level of pressure on the bankers we made  our problem their problem  and then here trenda kennedy who still  to this day is fighting to save her home  she led 750 everyday people to the  uh the home of a bank of america  executive it's kind of scary to do that  that's like breaking the be nice rule  the rules in society say you don't do  that  but we say hey bank of america comes to  the homes of everyday people  every single day and it's not pretty so  we're coming to you  and then these are just actions that  we've done and this is the kind of  activity that we're going to need to put  a new level of pressure  on those who've created the crisis so i  want to close with this  you build movements through a long-term  plan you build movements  through long-term thinking but today  if we want to change things for the  mitzi's for the ferals and for the  trenders we need the right now plan  and the right now plan starts with  moving people to action the right now  plan means tapping into a progressive  populist sentiment that is a simmer at  this moment  but we can blow it up into a boil the  right now man  plant means creating the arena for  people to move to action  to tune into their sense of somebodiness  and put a new level of pressure on those  who created the crisis we face today  thank you  okay so we're down to our final speaker  and  thank you guys all for being here and  hanging in this has been a really great  session i hope that you've enjoyed  it our next speaker and our final  speaker is deepak bhargava  deepak is executive director of center  for community change based in washington  d.c  he is a very inspiring leader to me um i  know him and i think he's great and he's  been at the center of the fight for  social justice and economic justice  policy change for over a decade most  recently he's been very active in the  fight for immigration reform  and for job creation he really is one of  the rising stars of the movement  and he has a great gift of kind of  wrapping it all together and breaking it  all down and so  i know you're going to love what he has  to say right now thanks a lot and  welcome deepak  good afternoon everybody  so i'm getting the feeling that one of  the main criteria for getting to speak  at netroots this is my first  is a prominent position on the glenn  beck wall  so i'm a little concerned about my own  status  for a while i was to the right of van  jones to the left of valerie jarrett and  underneath mount saitung  but i think i'm dropping so i'm hoping  my performance today kind of helps boost  my  boost my ratings so i'm deepak bhargava  i'm a community organizer and i'm proud  of it  what i'm going to try to do today i'm  going to try out some new material with  you today  is to talk about what might be some  principles  for a long-term progressive economic  vision  but before i do that i do want to talk  about a couple of things in the here and  now  so john maynard keynes the great  economist once said when  he was told that experts were expressing  confidence that in the long run  the economy would return to equilibrium  and full employment  kane said in the long run we're all dead  so we do live in a massive economic  crisis  and the first thing i want to say is we  need jobs desperately  we need a lot of them and we need them  right now  now the good news is we actually know  how to do this  we have had the experience in our  history of the 1930s with the works  progress administration the civilian  conservation corps  in the 1970s on a smaller scale with the  ceta program  and today there's a proposal that my  organization has been working on  uh that congressman george miller is  sponsored with 160 co-sponsors in the  house  that would create a hot would create a  million jobs a year for two years  for people to do useful work in  communities  greening the economy taking care of our  kids educating our kids  refurbishing schools and senator brown  sherrod brown is sponsored in the senate  we have the capacity in in the great  depression hundreds of thousands of jobs  were created in the span of months by  fdr and harry hopkins his key aid  in a time when there weren't it wasn't  the level of federal bureaucracy and  infrastructure that there is today  so we absolutely have the capacity to do  it and we should  the second thing i want to say is i want  to challenge you and ask you all  to join me and many many other  organizations who are calling a major  mobilization in washington  on october 2nd 2010 called the one  nation march  now i'm asking you to do this not just  because marching is fun  and i do think marching is fun but  because at this moment it is of great  strategic  importance there is a kind of malaise  and despondency and dispiritedness in  many parts of the progressive world  we have been barely able to get an  unemployment benefits extension through  the united states senate  let alone the kinds of ambitious wpa  style programs that i'm suggesting that  we really need to get the economy moving  without major mobilization we will not  be able  to turn the politics around on the  economy in this country so i want you to  come and to promote  10 to 10 as a major event  so let me move on to talk about the long  run so i work at the center for  community change whose mission is  to spur and support grassroots  organizing mostly in poor communities  and in communities of color  and we've done some great things over  the years but the hard truth of the  matter is  that the last 40 years since my  organization was founded in 1968  we've been living in a period of  retrenchment and reaction  on some basic issues of racial justice  of economic justice the gains of the new  deal the great society the civil rights  movement  have been steadily eroded over that  period of time  we've had a couple of important  victories in this last two years  uh the stimulus program the recovery act  though it's not  widely understood was the largest  expansion of anti-poverty programs in  this country in 40 years  the health care bill was the largest  redistribution  redistributive program in this country  in 40 years and the largest expansion of  the safety net  but the hard truth remains that for the  people who are the reason for my  organization's existence  life is worse not better we have 20  percent unemployment in communities of  color  in this country in some neighborhoods  it's a catastrophic levels of 50 percent  or higher  we have rising poverty extreme poverty  child poverty  increasing hunger increasing  homelessness there is immense  suffering in the united states of  america and  the brutal fact is that there's almost  nothing  in the mainstream economic or political  discourse today that would do a damn  thing about  that's the hard reality we start from  so in light of the seriousness of this  predicament  i want to take a crack at trying to  define what might that long-term vision  for a progressive economics in this  country be and i start from the premise  that we carry an albatross around our  necks  which is free market fundamentalism an  ideology that is so pervasive  that it is like the air we breathe and  the water we swim in we barely recognize  it  and as the british historian uh perry  anderson  said without a conceptual alternative  across the full range from the  philosophical to the technical to the  rally political  we will not be able to break through and  make progress  and some questions in our society have  been actually put beyond the pale of  acceptable discourse how much  wealth is too much wealth how much  inequality  is okay these questions are barely asked  anymore in our society and it was  not always so and it is also the case  that this is not just a problem of  conservative  ideology when a democratic president as  bill clinton  did signed welfare reform and destroyed  the safety net for poor families in this  country  and signed nafta to send millions of  american jobs overseas and at the same  time manage to impoverish  mexican people on the land in mexico  fueling the immigration that we see now  in our country  you know that this ideology is pervasive  and it is possible that in 2011 we will  have a fight  not just with republicans but with  democrats about saving and preserving  social security  so we have a deep deep ideological  problem  and i think that fighting these fights  simply on  issue grounds trade over here deficits  over there  safety nets over here jobs over there is  not sufficient to the task at hand  that the place to begin is at the level  of fundamental principles  now we all know the conservative  principles that operate through each and  every issue fight  that we see in congress and around the  country that  human beings are basically selfish that  competition  is the currency of american life that  wealth is created  principally by individual entrepreneurs  who are heroic  and that we all benefit when they do  well  that free markets are the best way to  organize an economy  that smallest government is the best  government  that we live in a colorblind society and  that racism is essentially  a thing of the past as we say as we new  yorkers say  forget about it what's an alternative  what's a path forward so i want to lay  out six potential  principles that we might think about and  some of these i hope will be a little  provocative  so first that wealth is not created  by individual heroic entrepreneurs  but wealth is created by all of us  so that activities that we sometimes  think about is  marginal or irrelevant economic  production scholarship  art science child care care giving these  are in fact  fundamentally constitutive of the  project of wealth building  so sometimes liberals will say well the  market system is the best we got we got  to support those entrepreneurs  but let's just take a little bit of that  money and give it to the poorest people  because the outcomes are so unfair  what i'm proposing is a different view  which is not that  they are the uh geese that lay the  golden eggs but that in fact  wealth is created by everyone in our  society that we all have a contribution  to it  and that we all therefore have a moral  stake in wealth  that is not a matter of charity that it  is a matter of recognition  of the productivity of workers of  communities of everybody and creating  wealth  second principle our shared  quality of life is more important than  the private accumulation of wealth  so sometimes the pursuit of private  accumulation of wealth serve social ends  and in that case it should be rewarded  but the speculative frenzy of the  housing bubble  uh the subprime crisis the ma the  excessive salaries and bonuses of  executives  this is not fundamentally productive  activity  capital that was locked up in lavish  mansions  in luxury vehicles and financial  speculation could have been used for  education  for transportation for clean energy and  other investments  that would have laid the groundwork for  long-term growth private wealth should  be respected  but it is not sacrosanct  third human beings are complex  and they are not exclusively driven by  selfishness  now progressives are not naive we don't  assume that people are always good  we did live with dick cheney for eight  years right so we have some evidence of  the contrary but we do assert and new  science backs this up that people  are capable of acting morally and with  care and compassion  for one another we are also unapologetic  in asserting that we have  obligations deep moral obligations  to one another to act and behave as  though other people  count as much as we do from these  principles we would generate a pretty  powerful critique of what's going on in  our country  today is it okay that some people are  walking around with a three million  dollar  gold ipod while other people are living  lives of quiet and invisible desperation  in our society  i think it's a moral outrage  and i think these kinds of behaviors of  the excess of wealth  are fundamentally sociopathic  they are destructive of a good society  and they ought to be condemned and  subject to cultural critique  fourth inequality  in wealth and income beyond a certain  point and we crossed this point long ago  in the united states  is destructive not just of democracy and  of a sense of a common project  but it also undermines the prospects of  a well-functioning economy that is to  say  more equality is good for  growth for a functioning  economy and that the inequality that  we've seen is not only  excessive bad on moral grounds  it actually undermines the conditions  for a good economy  fifth racial inequality is not a  separate issue from economic inequality  they are part and parcel of the same set  of structures that operate in the united  states of america  and any progressive project on economic  inequality given the  vast disparities of wealth and income  that we still see  will have to put racial justice at the  center of the project  last principle broader participation  by workers by community members is  good for economic productivity for  economic efficiency and therefore  we have a stake in promoting collective  organization of workers in unions and  supporting the efforts of community  organizers to build constituencies to  bargain for people  to struggle for a better life it is not  in against an opposition to a  well-functioning economy  it is actually a requirement of a  well-functioning economy  so if we took these values seriously  what might it imply  for an economic agenda for the country  well it would suggest that we might  still have  markets as a dominant feature of our  economic paradigm  but that we would structure those market  in such a way that they aligned with  social values  that might mean much higher standards  labor standards with respect to wages  and working conditions  sick days and paid vacation as  congressman grayson said  it might mean luxury taxes and estate  taxes so we don't see  outrageous concentrations of wealth it  might ask us  it might call on us to ask hard  questions  about the corporation and its role in  american life  the corporation is a relatively modern  invention  it did not come from god it is capable  of being  changed and reformed charter reforms  putting workers and community members on  boards  requiring there to be means of corporate  accountability greater regulation  all of these things become possible to  imagine  if we start from a different set of  values fundamentals  so i want to turn  finally to the issue that george engaged  george gale engaged so well  which is the question of social movement  because in american history shifts from  one set of values and principles to  another  have not come mainly through academic  exercises they have come  because millions of ordinary people have  taken history  into their own hands in the populist  movement  in the workers movements of the 1930s  and so forth so and the  projects of having an intellectual  agenda and ideas agenda on the one hand  and having a movement strategy on the  other are closely intertwined and linked  because if we don't have a story that we  believe in we can't build  a mass army for these changes but if we  don't have  a movement in the country we won't have  the power and we won't have the ideas  and the passion and the energy  from the grassroots to get it done so  i've had the luxury and the privilege  over the last  uh 10 years of being part of the  immigrant rights movement and i've  learned two things from that  one is that you've got a dream big when  we started no one thought that  legalizing 12 million undocumented  people was remotely feasible and not a  single person in the beltway wanted to  talk about it  similarly these kinds of economic  questions are going to need to be put on  the table  secondly we're going to need  organizations at scale  unions community groups perhaps new  forms of organization that we can't even  imagine today  to create the power and the engine for  those reforms  so let me just say in wrapping up that  um  i know this has seemed like this seems  in a way like a very perilous time  for the country and it is not going to  be easy to get some of the kinds of  reforms that were talked about here  today  but the truth is if it was easy it would  not be worthy of the progressive promise  because the progressive promise is  basically about keeping faith with the  unemployed worker in detroit the single  mom in fresno  the immigrant worker at a meat plant in  omaha with the belief  and the conviction that they are not  simply victims  but that we need their talent their  inspiration  linked together with all of us to build  a better america and we can do it and we  will do it  thank you very much  okay hi i've acquired a growth because  my husband had to go to his session this  is marco  and i think he's the youngest net roost  nation attendee ever  he's eight weeks um so anyway that's it  you guys thank you so much it was great  to have everybody here and thank you to  all the speakers who came and gave us  such  great food for thought thanks a lot take  care
 

